TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Preliminary Report (Part 1)","overview":"Plan a simple space mission that benefits Australia and present preliminary design work to guide the full mission assignment. Explain the mission concept and rationale, plan how the assignment will be completed, identify information sources and software, and show evidence of technical progress."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Mission concept and rationale — What will it do, who are the users, why is it needed?","weight":40,"max":40,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"A clear and complete description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Clearly relates required mission function to users and their needs. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Sufficient description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Identification of users and their needs. Descriptions feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"A poor description with mission goals, objectives and requirements unclear. Insufficient identification of users and their needs."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Project plan — How you plan to complete the assignment, including either a Gantt chart or a timetable and information sources you plan to use","weight":30,"max":30,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The plan for project completion is robust and has provision for project variations and contingencies. The plan fits within the narrative set out by the mission concept – the student makes clear why the plan is developed this way in the context of the mission. Appropriate references to information sources are included."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Plan is presented but lacks some detail. However, the plan is enough to believe that the project is feasible."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Plan is not included or does not have sufficient detail to demonstrate that the project can be completed."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Preliminary progress — Technical content and evidence of sufficient work done to date","weight":10,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Good preliminary set of results included with brief description and/or interpretation. Clearly on track for completion."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Minor work completed on the project. Results description or interpretation may be insufficient."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful work completed."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":20,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical structure. The report is very easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Mission Concept & Rationale"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan; Preliminary Progress"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan; Image 1"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan — Information sources"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Preliminary Progress"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"The proposed 10 m visible-band coral-colour imaging is not supported by a defined sensor or an assessment of water-column attenuation, depth, turbidity, cloud, and atmospheric correction; these factors can limit bleaching detection."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","issue":"The orbit calculations are broadly plausible, but the stated swath and equatorial ground-track shift do not demonstrate weekly access to the reef. The submission appropriately identifies coverage as unresolved and should verify it through access simulations and a repeat-cycle analysis."}],"overall":"A strong preliminary report with a well-defined Australian mission concept, a robust project plan, relevant sources, and credible initial orbit work. The main design challenge is demonstrating that the proposed optical observations can detect bleaching at the required resolution and revisit rate.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":40.0,"reasoning":"The mission purpose, measurable preliminary objectives, intended operator and customer groups, and their needs are clearly connected. The rationale is supported by relevant references. The optical payload and feasibility of detecting coral colour at the proposed resolution remain to be developed, so the concept is not yet fully validated.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"Give reef managers direct, repeatable observations of coral colour over the whole Great Barrier Reef, so that bleaching is detected within days instead of after a seasonal aerial survey."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"Objectives 1 and 3 come directly from the managers' need to react within days, and objective 2 from the researchers' need to see individual reefs."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":27.0,"max":30.0,"reasoning":"A clear Gantt chart is linked to the mission’s design sequence, with task descriptions, relevant information sources, and practical contingencies for access, coverage, schedule, and competing deadlines. The plan is robust and feasible; some technical decisions and task-level deliverables remain open, appropriately for this preliminary stage.","evidence":[{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"The order follows the mission: the imaging requirements (objectives 1 and 2) fix the orbit altitude and local time, so orbit work comes before ground control and communications, whose contact times depend on the chosen orbit."},{"where":"Project Plan — Contingencies","quote":"The main risks and the planned responses are listed in Table 1."},{"where":"Project Plan — Information sources","quote":"Table 2 lists the sources I plan to use and what each is for."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The report includes useful orbit calculations for altitude, period, orbit count, sun-synchronous inclination, ground-track shift, and swath, with an interpretation that identifies the coverage trade. The calculations are broadly sound and progress is evident, though the STK result is only briefly reported and no access or payload-performance analysis is yet shown.","evidence":[{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"I have also made first hand calculations for a candidate orbit, a circular sun-synchronous orbit at 600 km altitude (Table 3)"},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"A first STK scenario with this orbit has been set up, and its period agrees with the hand calculation."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a logical structure, readable prose, clearly labelled figures and tables, and a consistent overall presentation. In-text citations correspond to a reference list. Some online references are bibliographically vague or incomplete, and occasional awkward phrasing prevents the report from being fully polished.","evidence":[{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Figure 1: Project Gantt chart, Weeks 2 to 10 (gold = completed, blue = planned, red lines = report deadlines)."},{"where":"References","quote":"[7] J. R. Wertz, D. F. Everett, and J. J. Puschell, Eds., Space Mission Engineering: The New SMAD."}]}]}
